expr main <([look, looks, looked, looking] forward <(to talk), (talk)>), (talk)>

let message "After `look forward to`, use `talking`."
let description "In `look forward to`, the word `to` is a preposition, so a verb after it ends in `ing`."
let kind "Grammar"
let becomes "talking"

test "I look forward to talk you." "I look forward to talking you."
test "We are looking forward to talk the results." "We are looking forward to talking the results."
test "She looks forward to talk them." "She looks forward to talking them."
test "He looked forward to talk it." "He looked forward to talking it."
test "They look forward to talk us soon." "They look forward to talking us soon."
test "I really look forward to talk the team." "I really look forward to talking the team."
test "I look forward to talk the end of it." "I look forward to talking the end of it."
test "We look forward to talk more." "We look forward to talking more."

allows "I look forward to talking you."
allows "I want to talk you tomorrow."
allows "Look forward and talk it."
allows "We look forward to the talk."
allows "I look forward to your reply."
allows "I look forward to it."
